lesson:
A goblin, a troll and a dragon are all enemies. They all have a name and health, can take damage and can die. Writing that code three times means fixing every bug three times. **Inheritance** lets them share it:

```
public abstract class Enemy
{
    public string Name { get; }
    public int Health { get; protected set; }

    protected Enemy(string name, int health) { Name = name; Health = health; }

    public virtual void TakeDamage(int amount) => Health = Math.Max(0, Health - amount);
    public abstract int AttackDamage();
}

public class Troll : Enemy
{
    public Troll(string name) : base(name, 80) { }
    public override int AttackDamage() => 12;
    public override void TakeDamage(int amount) => base.TakeDamage(amount / 2);  // thick skin
}
```

The words, one at a time:
- `class Troll : Enemy` means a Troll **is an** Enemy. It gets everything Enemy has.
- `: base(name, 80)` calls the parent's constructor, so the shared setup happens in one place.
- `protected` means "this class and its children only". The Troll can set `Health`; outside code can't.
- `virtual` means "children may replace this". `override` is the child replacing it. `base.TakeDamage(...)` calls the parent's version, so the Troll reuses the rule instead of copying it.
- `abstract` on a method means "every child must write this". On a class it means "you can't make a plain Enemy, only a kind of enemy". That's right: there is no such thing as a generic enemy on screen.
- `sealed` means "nobody may inherit from this": use it for a boss that's final.

**The common mistake** is building deep family trees: `Enemy > FlyingEnemy > FireFlyingEnemy > ...`. Then you want a fire enemy that swims, and the tree breaks. A good test: use inheritance only for a real **is-a** relation, kept shallow. When it's really **has-a** (a player has health, a barrel has health), use **composition**: give each one a `Health` object instead of a shared parent.

That's exactly how Unity works. A GameObject *has* components (a Rigidbody, a Collider, your Health script) instead of inheriting from a giant base class. You'll still inherit (every script inherits `MonoBehaviour`), but you build behaviour by combining parts.

